---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/layout/Nav.astro';
---
<Base title="My Kid Has Better Identity Architecture Than Your Company" description="My son has four Roblox accounts and can explain the purpose of each one without thinking about it. That is a better identity design than most enterprises manage.">
<style>
:root {
  --bg:           #090908;
  --bg-2:         #0d0d0b;
  --bg-3:         #121210;
  --amber:        #c4972a;
  --amber-dim:    rgba(196,151,42,0.12);
  --amber-border: rgba(196,151,42,0.15);
  --teal:       #7eb8c4;
  --teal-dim:   rgba(126,184,196,.08);
  --teal-border:rgba(126,184,196,.12);
  --text:         #e8e6e1;
  --text-2:       #a09c94;
  --text-3:       #5a5752;
  --serif:        'DM Serif Display', Georgia, serif;
  --sans:         'DM Sans', system-ui, sans-serif;
  --mono:         'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
  --gutter:       clamp(24px, 5vw, 80px);
  --max:          1160px;
  --nav-h:        68px;
}
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 300; line-height: 1.7; -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; overflow-x: hidden; }
nav { position:fixed;top:0;left:0;right:0;z-index:200;height:var(--nav-h);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:0 var(--gutter);border-bottom:1px solid transparent;transition:all .4s; }
nav.scrolled { border-color:var(--amber-border);background:rgba(9,9,8,.93);backdrop-filter:blur(14px); }
.nav-logo { font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--text); }
.nav-logo span { color:var(--amber); }
.article { max-width:680px;margin:0 auto;padding:calc(var(--nav-h) + 80px) var(--gutter) 120px; }
.article-label { font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.6rem;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:20px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px; }
.article-label::before { content:'';width:20px;height:1px;background:var(--amber); }
h1 { font-family:var(--serif);font-size:clamp(2.1rem,4.6vw,3.3rem);font-weight:400;line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.025em;margin-bottom:32px; }
.article-meta { font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.62rem;color:var(--text-3);letter-spacing:.08em;margin-bottom:64px;padding-bottom:32px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--amber-border); }
.article p { color:var(--text-2);font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.9;margin-bottom:28px; }
.article p em { color:var(--text);font-style:italic; }
.article p strong { color:var(--text);font-weight:400; }
.article a { color:var(--amber);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid var(--amber-border); }
.article a:hover { border-bottom-color:var(--amber); }
.article h2 { font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.6rem;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:-.02em;margin:56px 0 20px;color:var(--text); }
.article blockquote { border-left:2px solid var(--amber);padding-left:24px;margin:40px 0;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;font-style:italic;color:var(--text-2);line-height:1.7; }
.article ul { list-style:none;margin:0 0 28px;padding:0; }
.article li { position:relative;padding-left:22px;margin-bottom:16px;color:var(--text-2);font-size:1.02rem;line-height:1.8; }
.article li::before { content:'';position:absolute;left:0;top:.85em;width:9px;height:1px;background:var(--amber); }
.article li strong { color:var(--text);font-weight:400; }
.article-footer { margin-top:80px;padding-top:40px;border-top:1px solid var(--amber-border);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between; }
.back-link { font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.65rem;color:var(--amber);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;text-decoration:none;border:none; }
</style>
<Nav variant="sub" backHref="/writing" backLabel="← Writing" />
<article class="article">
  <div class="article-label">Identity, September 2026</div>
  <h1>My Kid Has Better Identity Architecture Than Your Company</h1>
  <div class="article-meta">matterunknown &nbsp;·&nbsp; September 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 4 min read</div>

  <p>I asked my son how many Roblox accounts he has. He said four. Then he told me what each one was for, in order, without stopping to think about it.</p>

  <p>The main one, which his friends know. An alt, for playing things he does not want showing up on the main. One tied to a friend group he is not really in anymore. And one from when he was seven, whose password is gone forever, which still exists somewhere out there and is still, in some sense, him.</p>

  <p>Nobody taught him this. He has never read a word about scoped credentials. He arrived at it the way kids arrive at most things, which is by finding the alternative annoying.</p>

  <p>I have spent my working life on identity systems. It took me a second to admit what I was looking at. That is a better identity architecture than most companies I have worked with.</p>

  <h2>The adults got it backwards</h2>

  <p>Enterprise identity rests on a founding assumption nobody says out loud: one person, one identity, and everything else is a defect. The extra accounts even have a name in the industry. We call them <em>orphaned</em>. There are whole product categories devoted to finding your second account and killing it. We call that cleanup. Hygiene.</p>

  <p>My son would find this deranged. To him a second account is not debt, it is a tool. It exists because a particular context needed a particular face, and when the context ended the account went quiet instead of getting deleted. He is not confused about who he is. He is extremely clear about who he is. He is simply also clear that <em>who he is</em> and <em>which login he is using</em> are two different questions.</p>

  <blockquote>We spent twenty years trying to collapse people into one identity. He spent about ten minutes deciding that was a bad idea and moved on.</blockquote>

  <h2>Context collapse is obvious when you are eleven</h2>

  <p>Adults have a term for the thing where your boss, your mother, and your college roommate all see the same post. Context collapse. We talk about it like a modern affliction, something the platforms did to us.</p>

  <p>Children hit it early and route around it on instinct. School friends and internet friends are not the same audience. <a href="/writing/vibe-coding-roblox">The farming game we built together</a> is not for the kids who would give him grief about farming games. Different audience, different account. Done, and never discussed again.</p>

  <p>That is not deception. It is audience management, and every adult reading this already does it. You have a work email and a personal email and you know precisely which one gets the newsletter. Then you go into the office and help design a system that pretends people do not work that way.</p>

  <p>The difference between an eleven year old and an enterprise IAM rollout is not sophistication. It is timing. He sets his boundaries up front, deliberately, before anything exists. The enterprise discovers its boundaries three years later, in a spreadsheet, during an audit.</p>

  <h2>What he actually built</h2>

  <p>Take the Roblox out of it and here is the design, in the vocabulary he does not have yet:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Purpose binding.</strong> Every account exists for a reason he can state in one sentence.</li>
    <li><strong>Least privilege.</strong> Nothing an account can reach goes beyond what its purpose needs. The alt has no friends on it, so there is nothing on it to lose.</li>
    <li><strong>Blast radius.</strong> Something bad on one does not touch the others. Get griefed on the alt and the main never finds out.</li>
    <li><strong>Graceful decay.</strong> The dead ones are not deleted. They are dormant, and he knows they are dormant, which is a completely different thing from forgotten.</li>
    <li><strong>Correlation control.</strong> He decides who gets to know which of these are the same person.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>That last one is the sophisticated part. It is the piece standards bodies are still arguing about. He does it because it never occurred to him that it worked any other way.</p>

  <h2>Which brings me to agents</h2>

  <p>I spend my working days on the question of how an AI agent should be identified. The default answer, still, almost everywhere that has actually deployed something: give it a service account. One identity. Reused for everything it does. It queries the database, books the travel, and posts to the channel wearing the same face, and when something goes sideways you get a log line saying the agent did it, which is the least useful sentence in security.</p>

  <p>My son would ask why it does not just have four.</p>

  <p>He would be right. An agent doing four jobs, under four different intentions, with four different blast radiuses, is not one principal. It is four. The container is the same. <a href="/writing/the-workload-is-the-reasoning-session">The reasoning session is not.</a></p>

  <p>We do not build it that way because the tooling was written by people who believed a principal is a person, a person is one, and anything else is a mess to be reported on. It is not a hard problem. It is an inherited assumption, and it has been quietly wrong the entire time.</p>

  <h2>They are going to find us strange</h2>

  <p>He is not going to grow out of this. His whole generation will arrive at work having run several purpose-bound identities since before they could do long division, and they are going to find our systems bizarre.</p>

  <p>Good. They should.</p>

  <p>So the next time somebody tells you that scoped, short-lived, purpose-built, disposable identity is too complicated to roll out across the org, sit with this for a second: it is the default behavior of a child with a Chromebook and no supervision.</p>

  <p>We never made identity simple. We made it singular. Those are not the same thing, and the eleven year old in my house worked that out before we did.</p>

